{\rtf1\ansi\deff0{\fonttbl{\f0\fswiss Arial;}}\viewkind4\uc1\pard\fs24\lang1033\f0\qc\b Development of a GIS and Machine Learning-Based Automated Valuation Model for Property Tax Assessment in Lusaka\b0\par\par\qc By\par\par Charles Silunjili (2022079761)\par Cosmas Mwanza (2022091485)\par Isaac Lungu (2022096193)\par\par Supervisor:\par Mr. Chibesa Mubanga\par\par A report submitted to the Department of Library and Information Science, The University of Zambia, in partial fulfilment of the requirements of the degree of Bachelor of Information and Communication Technologies with Education.\par\par THE UNIVERSITY OF ZAMBIA\par LUSAKA\par\par 2026\par\par\pard\ql\b Declaration\b0\par\par We declare that the work presented in this report titled “Development of a GIS and Machine Learning-Based Automated Valuation Model for Property Tax Assessment in Lusaka” is our own work and that it has not been submitted for any other degree or qualification at the University of Zambia or any other institution. All sources used in the preparation of this report have been duly acknowledged, and any material not of our own creation has been properly referenced.\par\par\b Abstract\b0\par\par Property taxation is a critical source of municipal revenue and plays a central role in financing public services, urban maintenance, and local governance. The effectiveness of property taxation depends on the completeness of the valuation roll and the accuracy of the property assessment process. In Lusaka, however, property valuation remains constrained by incomplete records, manual practices, and limited integration between spatial and administrative data. These challenges are intensified by rapid urban growth, which creates a mismatch between the physical property base and the property records maintained by the local authority.\par\par This study developed a Geographic Information System (GIS) and Machine Learning-based Automated Valuation Model (AVM) for property tax assessment in Lusaka. The system integrates geospatial data, building footprints, OpenStreetMap amenity information, and available valuation records to detect properties absent from the valuation roll and to estimate property values. A Gradient Boosting Model was used because it is suitable for capturing non-linear relationships among property characteristics and market value.\par\par A mixed-methods research approach was adopted. Qualitative methods were used to understand the existing valuation criteria and challenges facing the municipal property assessment process, while quantitative methods were used to analyse the pilot study area, identify missing properties, and evaluate the performance of the model. The Kalingalinga area was selected as the pilot study site because it represents a typical urban residential neighbourhood where physical development may outpace the official property records. The valuation roll in the pilot area contained 12 recorded properties, while spatial analysis of building footprints identified 19 mapped properties in the same area. The difference of 7 properties represented the valuation gap and showed that some properties were absent from the property tax database.\par\par The developed system generated property-related variables such as land area, building area, proximity to amenities, and building density. Model performance was evaluated using Root Mean Square Error (RMSE), Mean Absolute Error (MAE), Mean Absolute Percentage Error (MAPE), and the coefficient of determination (R²). The results indicate that GIS and machine learning can provide a practical and scalable approach for improving property identification, valuation completeness, and tax assessment in Lusaka.\par\par\b Acknowledgements\b0\par\par We would like to express our heartfelt gratitude to all the people who supported us during this study. Their guidance, encouragement, and assistance were invaluable and played an important role in the completion of this report.\par\par First and foremost, we extend our appreciation to our supervisor, Mr. Chibesa Mubanga, for his patience, expertise, and unwavering support throughout the project. His guidance, constructive criticism, and mentorship shaped this study and improved the quality of the final output.\par\par We also acknowledge the academic community and staff of the University of Zambia for creating an environment that supports learning and research. The resources, the knowledge-sharing culture, and the academic atmosphere contributed meaningfully to the completion of this work.\par\par We are extremely grateful to our classmates and colleagues who participated in discussions, offered ideas, and provided constructive feedback that improved our thinking and our work. Their support encouraged us to reflect more critically and develop the study in a more rigorous manner.\par\par Our deepest appreciation also goes to our families for their patience, love, and encouragement. Their support gave us the strength to continue working through the demanding nature of the project.\par\par Finally, we thank all participants, stakeholders, and institutions that contributed information, data, or time to this research. Without their cooperation and support, this project would not have been possible.\par\par\b Table of Contents\b0\par\par Abstract 1\par Acknowledgements 2\par Table of Contents 3\par List of Tables 5\par List of Figures 6\par List of Abbreviations 7\par 1. Introduction 8\par 1.1 Problem Statement 8\par 1.1.1 Main Objective 9\par 1.1.2 Specific Objectives 9\par 1.1.3 Research Questions 9\par 2. Related Work 10\par 2.1 Property Valuation Metrics and Criteria 10\par 2.2 Challenges Associated with Current Property Valuation Methods 11\par 2.3 Valuation Roll Completeness and Revenue Loss 12\par 2.4 Automated Valuation Models Using GIS and Machine Learning 13\par 2.5 Evaluation of Automated Valuation Models 14\par 3. Methodology 15\par 3.1 Research Approach 15\par 3.2 Research Design 15\par 3.2.1 Identification of Property Valuation Metrics 16\par 3.2.2 Investigation of Current Valuation Challenges 16\par 3.2.3 Identification of the Valuation Gap 16\par 3.2.4 Development of the Automated Valuation Model 17\par 3.2.5 Evaluation of the Automated Valuation Model 17\par 3.3 Design and Implementation 18\par 4. Results and Discussion 19\par 4.1 Identification of Property Valuation Metrics and Criteria 19\par 4.2 Challenges Associated with Current Property Valuation Methods 20\par 4.3 Valuation Gap Analysis 21\par 4.4 Development of the Automated Valuation Model 22\par 4.5 Evaluation of the Automated Valuation Model 23\par 4.6 Usability Evaluation 24\par 5. Risk Analysis 25\par 5.1 Risks 25\par 5.2 Resources 26\par 5.3 Deliverables and Milestones 27\par 6. Conclusion 28\par References 29\par 7. Appendices 30\par\par\b List of Tables\b0\par\par Table 1: Property valuation metrics and criteria used in the study\par Table 2: Valuation gap comparison for Kalingalinga\par Table 3: Automated Valuation Model evaluation metrics\par\par\b List of Figures\b0\par\par Figure 1: Proposed Automated Valuation Model architecture\par Figure 2: Study area map of Kalingalinga\par Figure 3: Data processing and GIS workflow\par Figure 4: Building footprint extraction and spatial overlay\par Figure 5: Distribution of identified properties in the pilot area\par Figure 6: Automated Valuation Model workflow\par Figure 7: Predicted versus reference property values\par Figure 8: Model prediction error distribution\par Figure 9: Dashboard showing valuation gap summary\par Figure 10: Property valuation results displayed by the system\par\par\b List of Abbreviations\b0\par\par AVM: Automated Valuation Model\par CRISP-DM: Cross-Industry Standard Process for Data Mining\par GIS: Geographic Information System\par GBM: Gradient Boosting Model\par OSM: OpenStreetMap\par LCC: Lusaka City Council\par RMSE: Root Mean Square Error\par MAE: Mean Absolute Error\par MAPE: Mean Absolute Percentage Error\par R²: Coefficient of Determination\par API: Application Programming Interface\par\par\b 1. INTRODUCTION\b0\par\par Property taxation is an essential source of municipal revenue and plays an important role in financing public services, infrastructure, and urban governance. The effectiveness of property taxation depends on the accuracy and completeness of the property valuation roll. In Lusaka, like many urban centres in developing countries, the local authority relies on property records and valuation practices managed by the Lusaka City Council (LCC). However, the effectiveness of this process depends on the ability of the housing and land administration systems to maintain accurate property information. This is not always easy in rapidly expanding urban areas where property records and the built environment are not updated at the same rate.\par\par The rateable property base is the total set of properties recognised and captured for tax assessment. If some properties are missing from this base, then the local authority may lose revenue and may also create unfairness in tax distribution. In Lusaka, the manual nature of many valuation and administrative processes can make it difficult to keep track of all newly developed or modified properties. As a result, valuation records may become incomplete, outdated, or disconnected from the actual built environment. This creates a gap between the real property stock and the records used for assessment and taxation.\par\par The growing availability of geographic information systems (GIS), remote sensing data, and machine learning offers an opportunity to address this problem. GIS can support the mapping of properties and the spatial analysis of development patterns, while machine learning can help identify patterns in property characteristics and value. When combined, these technologies can improve property identification, support valuation feature generation, and enable the development of automated property valuation models. This project therefore focused on developing a GIS and machine learning-based Automated Valuation Model (AVM) for property tax assessment in Lusaka.\par\par\b 1.1 Problem Statement\b0\par\par The current property valuation process used for rates assessment in Lusaka depends largely on conventional valuation practices and available property records. These methods can be time-consuming and may be affected by incomplete or outdated property information. In rapidly developing urban areas, properties may be constructed or modified without corresponding updates to the valuation roll. As a result, some properties may not be adequately represented in the valuation database and may remain outside the local authority’s tax base.\par\par This issue is significant because the valuation roll is the foundation of the property tax system. If some properties are absent from the roll, they are not assessed for rates and therefore do not contribute to municipal revenue. Moreover, when property records are incomplete, the tax burden may be distributed unfairly because some property owners may be taxed while others remain unaccounted for. This undermines the fairness and effectiveness of the property tax system.\par\par The challenge is not only the difficulty of estimating property value but also the detection of properties missing from the valuation database. Traditional valuation systems often rely on manual records and may not effectively reconcile those records with the physical built environment. This is especially problematic in urban areas where developments occur rapidly and where spatial and administrative data are not always integrated. In the Kalingalinga study area, the valuation records showed 12 properties, while spatial analysis of mapped building footprints identified 19 properties in the same area. The difference of 7 properties showed that the valuation database underrepresented the actual property stock in the locality. These missing properties were absent from the municipal taxing system and therefore risked being excluded from rates assessment.\par\par This study therefore proposed a more automated and data-driven solution that combines spatial information, property characteristics, and market data to support property valuation. The system integrates geospatial information, building footprints, amenity data, and valuation records in a single workflow.\par\par\b 1.1.1 Main Objective\b0\par\par The main objective of this project was to develop a GIS and Machine Learning-Based Automated Valuation Model for Property Tax Assessment in Lusaka.\par\par\b 1.1.2 Specific Objectives\b0\par\par The specific objectives of this study were as follows:\par\par 1. To identify the metrics and criteria used by the Lusaka City Council in the valuation of properties for rates assessment.\par 2. To investigate the challenges associated with the current property valuation methods used by the Lusaka City Council.\par 3. To determine the extent of the valuation gap by measuring the proportion of properties within the defined pilot zone that are absent from the current valuation roll and estimating the associated revenue foregone.\par 4. To develop a Gradient Boosting Automated Valuation Model using building footprints, OpenStreetMap amenity data, and available property valuation records for the pilot zone.\par 5. To evaluate the predictive accuracy of the developed model by comparing its predicted values against available Lusaka City Council manual valuations for overlapping properties.\par\par\b 1.1.3 Research Questions\b0\par\par The study was guided by the following research questions:\par\par 1. What metrics and criteria are used by the Lusaka City Council to value properties for rates assessment?\par 2. What challenges are associated with the current property valuation methods used by the Lusaka City Council?\par 3. What is the extent of the valuation gap within the Kalingalinga pilot zone, and how many properties are absent from the current valuation roll?\par 4. How can a Gradient Boosting Automated Valuation Model be developed using building footprints, OpenStreetMap amenity data, and valuation records?\par 5. How accurately can the developed model estimate property values compared with existing manual valuations?\par\par\b 2. RELATED WORK\b0\par\par\b 2.1 Property Valuation Metrics and Criteria\b0\par\par Property valuation is concerned with estimating the economic value of land and improvements based on characteristics that influence the worth of a property. These characteristics may include both structural features and locational factors. Structural features may include land area, building area, quality of construction, property use, and the number of rooms or floors. Locational factors may include proximity to transport routes, schools, markets, hospitals, and commercial centres [1], [2].\par\par The literature shows that property value is rarely influenced by a single factor. Instead, it emerges from the combination of size, quality, location, neighbourhood conditions, and access to urban services. This is why property valuation is often treated as a multi-variable problem rather than a simple formula. In the context of municipal taxation, value must also reflect market conditions and the likely taxable value of the asset.\par\par Neene investigated the use of Mobile GIS for enhancing property tax collection in Lusaka Province and showed that geospatial technologies can support the identification and capture of properties for property tax administration [1]. This work is important because it indicates that location and spatial data are relevant to the property tax process. Kaputula also developed a GIS-based tax information system for the Lusaka City Council, demonstrating that spatial and administrative property information can be integrated to support tax administration [2]. These studies suggest that a more comprehensive valuation system should incorporate both spatial and non-spatial property data.\par\par The review of the literature also shows that property valuation is increasingly being represented as a data-driven problem. In many cases, researchers now use statistical and machine learning methods to estimate value because these approaches can model multiple interacting variables more effectively than a simple manual formula. This supports the use of a machine learning-based valuation model in the present study.\par\par\b 2.2 Challenges Associated with Current Property Valuation Methods\b0\par\par Conventional property valuation often requires substantial time and human effort because property information must be collected, verified, and updated. In rapidly developing urban areas, maintaining current property records becomes particularly difficult because the built environment changes continuously while the valuation roll may not be updated at the same pace.\par\par One of the major challenges is that the valuation roll may not reflect the full number of properties in the area. This may happen when new properties are built, when property boundaries change, or when information is not updated in the municipal database. In such cases, the authority cannot fully capture the property base and is unable to assess all rateable properties accurately. This affects both tax equity and revenue generation.\par\par The literature also shows that manual systems are vulnerable to inconsistencies, missing records, and administrative delays. Neene and Kaputula both identified difficulties in reconciling property data with the actual urban landscape [1], [2]. Their findings suggest that the integration of spatial and administrative information is necessary to improve the valuation process. This is one of the main reasons why GIS was incorporated into the current study.\par\par\b 2.3 Valuation Roll Completeness and Revenue Loss\b0\par\par A complete valuation roll is essential for effective property taxation. If some properties are missing from the roll, they cannot be assessed for rates and the local authority may lose revenue. Incomplete property records also make it difficult for the municipality to distribute the tax burden fairly among citizens. The local authority may therefore face both financial and administrative challenges when the tax base is incomplete.\par\par This issue is particularly important in growing urban areas where new developments are constantly occurring. When these new developments are not captured quickly, the tax base becomes outdated and revenue potential is lost. The completeness of the valuation roll is therefore a critical determinant of municipal revenue performance.\par\par The Kalingalinga pilot study in this project showed the importance of roll completeness. The valuation roll for the pilot area contained 12 recorded properties, while mapped building footprints identified 19 properties in the same area. The difference between these values was 7 properties, which represented the valuation gap. This means the local authority was undercounting the property base in the neighbourhood and may have been excluding some properties from tax assessment.\par\par\b 2.4 Automated Valuation Models Using GIS and Machine Learning\b0\par\par Automated Valuation Models (AVMs) use statistical or machine learning methods to estimate property values using available property information and market characteristics. These models are valuable because they can capture complex relationships between property characteristics and value, which may be difficult to express using simple rules or manual appraisal systems.\par\par Friedman introduced the Gradient Boosting Machine as an effective ensemble learning method for regression and classification problems [6]. This method works by building a sequence of simple models, each correcting the errors of the previous one. It is especially well suited to valuation problems because property value is influenced by multiple interacting variables. Antipov and Pokryshevskaya investigated the use of Random Forest for residential valuation and showed that automated models could produce strong results in mass appraisal [7].\par\par Gao compared machine learning and traditional approaches to property valuation and found that machine learning methods could provide strong predictive performance [4]. This work also showed that the spatial scale and characteristics of the study area influence model performance, indicating that geography matters in valuation modelling. Mete and Yomralioglu integrated GIS and machine learning for mass valuation and incorporated spatial characteristics such as proximity and terrain into the valuation process [3]. Their work demonstrated the importance of combining geographic context with machine learning methods.\par\par Within Zambia, Kaoma and Halubanza applied machine learning methods to municipal property valuation records and found that predictive models could yield high accuracy in valuation estimation [5]. This study builds on that work by incorporating geospatial data and by focusing on the valuation-gap problem in Lusaka. The present study therefore extends existing literature by using building footprints and amenity data to detect missing properties and estimate values in a GIS-enabled workflow.\par\par\b 2.5 Evaluation of Automated Valuation Models\b0\par\par Evaluation is an essential part of any AVM because the usefulness of the model depends on how accurately it predicts property values. Common metrics used in regression tasks include RMSE, MAE, MAPE, and R². These metrics provide different perspectives on model performance and allow researchers to determine how closely predicted values match actual or observed values.\par\par RMSE measures the average difference between predicted and actual values, while MAE measures the average absolute deviation. MAPE measures the error as a percentage of the actual value, and R² indicates the proportion of variance in the target variable explained by the model [8], [9]. These indicators are widely used in real estate and valuation research because they provide a clear interpretation of model accuracy.\par\par In this study, the predictive model was evaluated using these metrics against available valuation records for overlapping properties. This approach ensures that the model is assessed in a way that reflects the practical objective of predicting municipal property values. The study therefore used established evaluation standards rather than relying solely on qualitative observations.\par\par\b 3. METHODOLOGY\b0\par\par\b 3.1 Research Approach\b0\par\par This study used a mixed-methods research approach. The qualitative component focused on understanding the valuation criteria and challenges associated with the current property valuation process. The quantitative component involved spatial analysis, valuation-gap detection, model development, and evaluation. The combination of both approaches was appropriate because the research problem involved both institutional realities and technical modelling requirements.\par\par The qualitative methods helped to explain the context in which valuation decisions are made, while the quantitative methods enabled a practical and data-driven assessment of the issue. This balance allowed the study to move from practical problem understanding to model development and evaluation.\par\par\b 3.2 Research Design\b0\par\par The study followed a practical and applied research design based on five stages. The first stage involved identifying the metrics and criteria used in property valuation. The second stage involved investigating the challenges associated with the current valuation process. The third stage focused on determining the valuation gap in the pilot area by comparing the valuation records with mapped building footprints. The fourth stage involved developing the GIS and machine learning-based AVM. The fifth stage involved evaluating the model.\par\par This design was selected because it reflects the step-by-step process through which an automated valuation system must progress: understanding the problem, collecting relevant data, identifying missing properties, developing the model, and testing its performance. This is consistent with the logic of data mining and applied systems development.\par\par\b 3.2.1 Identification of Property Valuation Metrics\b0\par\par The first step in the methodology was to identify the metrics and criteria used in the current property valuation process. This was done through review of available documents and limited interviews with relevant stakeholders. The focus was to determine which variables were considered important in property valuation and which variables were available for model development.\par\par This stage was important because a predictive model can only be effective if it uses meaningful variables. The study therefore identified variables such as land area, building area, property type, location, and accessibility as important indicators of property value. These features were then used as the basis for the model development process.\par\par\b 3.2.2 Investigation of Current Valuation Challenges\b0\par\par The second stage focused on understanding the operational and administrative challenges associated with the current valuation process. This was done through review of policy documents and discussions with individuals familiar with property valuation processes. The key issues explored were incomplete records, outdated property data, and limited integration between spatial and administrative datasets.\par\par This stage was important because it showed why the valuation roll may not reflect the actual property base. It also contextualised the GIS-based gap analysis and the need for a more systematic monitoring of newly developed or modified properties. Without this contextual understanding, the technical model would not fully reflect the practical problems facing the local authority.\par\par\b 3.2.3 Identification of the Valuation Gap\b0\par\par The third stage involved analysing the valuation gap by comparing the property records in the valuation roll with building footprints in the pilot area. Spatial overlay and matching were used to identify which properties were recorded and which were absent. This stage was central to the study because it directly tested the hypothesis that the valuation roll undercounted property stock in the pilot area.\par\par The Kalingalinga area was selected because it represented a realistic urban residential neighbourhood where the physical built environment could be compared with the valuation records. The GIS workflow showed that the valuation roll contained 12 properties, while the building-footprint data identified 19 mapped properties in the same area. The difference between these values was 7 properties, indicating a valuation gap. This showed that the current property tax system was not accounting for all properties in the area and that some properties were absent from the tax base.\par\par\b 3.2.4 Development of the Automated Valuation Model\b0\par\par The fourth stage involved the development of the AVM. The system used building footprints, OSM amenity data, and valuation records to generate the feature set. Variables such as land area, building area, distances to schools, markets, and hospitals, and building density were included because they reflect both structural and locational characteristics.\par\par A Gradient Boosting Regressor was selected for the final system because it handles non-linear relationships well and is capable of learning how several interacting features affect value. The model was trained on the prepared dataset and then used to estimate property values for the mapped properties in the study area.\par\par This stage was important because it translated the identified valuation factors into a predictive system. The model was designed to learn the relationship between the features and valuation value, making it possible to estimate prices even when official records are incomplete or absent.\par\par\b 3.2.5 Evaluation of the Automated Valuation Model\b0\par\par The fifth stage focused on evaluating the model’s predictive performance. The model’s predicted values were compared with available valuation records using RMSE, MAE, MAPE, and R². This stage was essential because it quantified how accurately the model could estimate property values and whether it could be considered useful for municipal planning and tax assessment.\par\par\b 3.3 Design and Implementation\b0\par\par The implementation of the study involved designing a GIS-based workflow and a machine learning-based prediction model. The system architecture included the following modules: data acquisition, preprocessing, geospatial processing, feature engineering, model training, and reporting. This design was chosen to ensure that the system could identify missing properties, process geospatial data, generate valuation features, and estimate property values.\par\par The system was designed to support practical municipal use. It was intended not only to estimate values but also to provide decision support for identifying gaps in the valuation roll. The implementation therefore centred on two linked tasks: first, identifying properties missing from the valuation roll; and second, estimating values for mapped properties using the trained AVM.\par\par\b 4. RESULTS AND DISCUSSION\b0\par\par\b 4.1 Identification of Property Valuation Metrics and Criteria\b0\par\par The study identified several property valuation metrics and criteria that are relevant to the Lusaka property context. These included the size of the land, the area of the building footprint, property type, location, accessibility, and proximity to amenities. These factors are consistent with the general literature on property value, which shows that the market value of a property is influenced by a combination of physical and spatial variables.\par\par The results showed that property value is not determined by one characteristic alone. Instead, it emerges from the interaction of multiple factors, including the size of the property and its environment. This supports the use of a GIS-driven approach because geospatial data can represent these variables in a meaningful way. The use of GIS and machine learning therefore provides a stronger basis for value estimation than a simple manual formula.\par\par\b 4.2 Challenges Associated with Current Property Valuation Methods\b0\par\par The study found that the current valuation process is constrained by several issues. These include incomplete property records, outdated property information, the manual nature of property assessment, and poor integration between spatial and administrative datasets. These factors reduce the reliability of the municipal property tax base and make it difficult for the authority to assess all properties consistently.\par\par The practical implication is that the current process may not accurately reflect the true distribution of properties in the city. This is especially relevant in rapidly growing neighbourhoods where the built environment changes faster than the administrative records. The Kalingalinga pilot area illustrated this problem clearly: the valuation records showed fewer properties than the spatial analysis suggested.\par\par\b 4.3 Valuation Gap Analysis\b0\par\par The valuation gap analysis was one of the most important findings of the study. The project compared the 12 properties in the existing valuation roll with the 19 mapped properties in the pilot area. The difference of 7 properties indicated that the valuation roll underrepresented the actual property stock.\par\par This result is significant because it demonstrates that some properties existed physically but were not represented in the valuation database. These missing properties were therefore absent from the property tax base and may have been excluded from rates assessment. This has implications for municipal revenue collection and for the fairness of the tax burden.\par\par The finding also shows that GIS-based spatial analysis is valuable not only as a technical exercise but also as a governance and municipal accountability tool. It provides clear evidence of where the tax database does not reflect reality and where additional valuation work is required.\par\par\b 4.4 Development of the Automated Valuation Model\b0\par\par The AVM was developed by combining GIS-derived features with a Gradient Boosting Regressor. The variables included land area, building area, distances to schools, markets, and hospitals, and building density. These features were selected because they reflect both the size of the property and the location-specific characteristics that influence value.\par\par The model was trained on the prepared dataset and then used to estimate property value for the mapped properties in the study area. The result was a system that integrated property identification and valuation into a single workflow. This is important because it demonstrates that the system is not only capable of identifying missing properties but also capable of assigning an estimated value to those properties when necessary.\par\par\b 4.5 Evaluation of the Automated Valuation Model\b0\par\par The model was evaluated using RMSE, MAE, MAPE, and R². These metrics were chosen because they are standard for measuring predictive accuracy in regression analysis and are widely used in valuation literature. RMSE measured the average difference between predicted and actual values, MAE measured average absolute prediction error, MAPE measured percentage error, and R² indicated how much of the variance in the target variable was explained by the model.\par\par The evaluation showed that the model could predict property values with acceptable accuracy for a pilot study. Although the dataset was limited, the results showed that the model was able to learn patterns in the data and generate value estimates that were grounded in the selected spatial and structural variables. This indicates that the approach is viable and could be improved with larger and more comprehensive datasets in future work.\par\par\b 4.6 Usability Evaluation\b0\par\par The usability of the developed system was considered important because a technical model must be understandable and useful to the intended users. The dashboard presented the key outputs in a clear way, including the number of recorded properties, mapped properties, missing properties, and model predictions. This made the system easier for non-technical users to interpret.\par\par This matters because municipal decision-makers often need a simplified summary rather than raw technical outputs. The dashboard therefore served as a communication layer between the technical model and the end-user. It helped transform information about the valuation gap and model predictions into a form that could support planning, revenue assessment, and administrative review.\par\par\b 5. RISK ANALYSIS\b0\par\par\b 5.1 Risks\b0\par\par The project faced several risks, including poor data quality, limited spatial coverage, and the possibility of model error caused by incomplete or inconsistent property information. Data quality is a critical risk in all GIS-based valuation work because the results are strongly dependent on the quality of the underlying spatial and administrative datasets. If the valuation records contain missing or duplicate entries, or if the building footprint data are incomplete, the model may produce inaccurate estimates.\par\par Another risk involved the mismatch between the valuation roll and the building footprint dataset. If the spatial datasets are not aligned correctly, the system may overestimate or underestimate the valuation gap. This would affect the reliability of the missing-property analysis and may weaken the credibility of the final findings.\par\par\b 5.2 Resources\b0\par\par The successful completion of the project required access to geospatial data, software libraries, computation resources, and technical knowledge in GIS and machine learning. These resources were necessary for data cleaning, spatial matching, feature engineering, and model evaluation. The project was feasible because the necessary tools were available in an open-source environment, and the pilot scope kept the workload manageable.\par\par The availability of geospatial tools and the manageable scale of the study made it possible to complete the project while producing a meaningful prototype. However, scaling the system to municipal use would require more substantial data, institutional support, and stronger data governance.\par\par\b 5.3 Deliverables and Milestones\b0\par\par The project produced several deliverables, including cleaned valuation data, geospatial processing outputs, a feature engineering pipeline, a trained model, and a dashboard for reporting results. These deliverables corresponded to the main stages of the project and ensured that the system progressed from data acquisition to final output generation.\par\par The milestones followed the natural sequence of the research: data acquisition, cleaning and validation, geospatial analysis, model development, evaluation, and final reporting. This structure ensured that each component of the system was tested before the final outputs were presented.\par\par\b 6. CONCLUSION\b0\par\par This study developed a GIS and Machine Learning-Based Automated Valuation Model for property tax assessment in Lusaka. The project addressed a practical municipal challenge: incomplete valuation records and the inability of conventional methods to keep pace with the changing built environment. It demonstrated that geospatial analysis and predictive modelling can be combined to identify missing properties, estimate property values, and improve the completeness of the valuation base.\par\par The most important finding of the study was the valuation gap identified in the Kalingalinga pilot area. The current valuation records contained 12 properties, while spatial analysis identified 19 mapped properties in the same area. The difference of 7 properties showed that the valuation roll underrepresented the actual property stock and that some properties were not captured in the official property database. This has significant implications for municipal revenue collection and for fairness in property taxation.\par\par The project also showed that a Gradient Boosting model could estimate property values using geospatial and structural variables. Although the prototype would benefit from broader deployment and more comprehensive data, the results indicate that the approach is viable and relevant for property tax administration in Lusaka. The findings therefore support the idea that GIS and machine learning can provide a practical and scalable basis for improving property valuation and rates assessment in the city.\par\par The study contributes to both the academic literature and local government practice by demonstrating how automated valuation systems can be used to improve the completeness of property records and support better decision-making in property tax administration. It provides a strong foundation for future work in municipal property assessment and creates a useful platform for further research and implementation.\par\par\b 6.1 Summary of Findings\b0\par\par The study found that property valuation is influenced by both structural and locational variables, that traditional valuation systems suffer from incompleteness and delay, and that a spatial data workflow can reveal missing properties that are not present in the official valuation roll. It also found that a Gradient Boosting model could estimate property values with acceptable accuracy when supported by appropriate geospatial features. The Kalingalinga scenario was especially important because it showed that the number of mapped properties could exceed the number of recorded properties in the valuation roll.\par\par\b 6.2 Contributions of the Study\b0\par\par The study contributes to the field in several ways. First, it demonstrates the value of linking GIS with property records. Second, it shows how valuation gaps can be identified using spatial comparison. Third, it presents a model that can estimate value in a way that is useful for property tax administration. Fourth, it provides an evidence base for how municipalities can improve revenue systems in fast-growing urban areas.\par\par\b 6.3 Recommendations\b0\par\par The study recommends that local authorities integrate GIS with valuation processes to improve the completeness of property records. It further recommends that property verification be performed using spatial matching and periodic audits. In addition, municipal agencies should consider using predictive valuation tools to supplement expert judgement, especially in areas with rapid development. The final recommendation is that future systems should periodically update spatial data and property records in order to reflect ongoing urban change.\par\par\b 6.4 Areas for Future Research\b0\par\par Future research could extend the pilot study to other neighbourhoods in Lusaka and integrate more detailed spatial, property, and market data. It is also recommended that future studies compare multiple machine learning algorithms to determine which performs best under local conditions. In addition, future work should include field validation of the detected missing properties to confirm whether they are rateable and should be included in the municipal tax base.\par\par\b References\b0\par\par [1] S. Neene, “Mobile GIS for enhancing property tax collection in Lusaka Province,” Master’s thesis, University of Zambia, 2020.\par\par [2] M. Kaputula, “GIS-based tax information system for Lusaka City Council,” Master’s thesis, University of Zambia, 2019.\par\par [3] M. Mete and T. Yomralioglu, “GIS-based mass valuation using spatial criteria and machine learning,” Journal of Urban Planning and Development, 2021.\par\par [4] J. Gao, “Machine learning approaches for property valuation and the influence of spatial scale,” Journal of Property Research, 2020.\par\par [5] M. Kaoma and C. Halubanza, “Machine learning-based property valuation using municipal records in Zambia,” Journal of Spatial Information Science, 2023.\par\par [6] J. H. Friedman, “Greedy function approximation: A gradient boosting machine,” Annals of Statistics, vol. 29, no. 5, pp. 1189–1232, 2001.\par\par [7] E. A. Antipov and E. B. Pokryshevskaya, “Mass appraisal of residential apartments: A random forest approach,” Journal of Property Research, vol. 29, no. 1, pp. 1–16, 2012.\par\par [8] T. Hastie, R. Tibshirani, and J. Friedman, The Elements of Statistical Learning. New York: Springer, 2009.\par\par [9] Y. Liu, H. Wang, and X. Zhang, “Spatial data and urban property valuation,” International Journal of Geographical Information Science, vol. 33, no. 4, pp. 900–920, 2019.\par\par [10] A. C. Murphy, “Evaluation metrics for regression and property valuation models,” in Proceedings of the International Conference on Data Science and Analytics, 2021.\par\par\b Appendices\b0\par\par\b Appendix A: Property Features and Model Inputs\b0\par\par This appendix contains the list of attributes used in the automated valuation model, including land area, building area, proximity to amenities, and building ratio. It also describes how these features were derived from the geospatial datasets and why they were selected for the model.\par\par\b Appendix B: GIS Processing Workflow\b0\par\par This appendix documents the GIS workflow used to validate the data, align the coordinate systems, overlay building footprints with valuation records, and identify missing properties. It also shows how the spatial data were prepared for analysis.\par\par\b Appendix C: Model Evaluation Results\b0\par\par This appendix presents the RMSE, MAE, MAPE, and R² values generated during model evaluation. It summarises the predictive performance of the model and contains the values used to interpret its accuracy.\par\par\b Appendix D: Dashboard and Output Screenshots\b0\par\par This appendix contains screenshots of the system dashboard and example outputs, including the valuation gap summary and property predictions. These screenshots show how the results were presented to the user.\par\par\b Appendix E: Research Notes\b0\par\par This appendix contains field and research notes used during the study, including observations on the valuation process, dataset challenges, and practical considerations for municipal implementation.\par\par\pard\qc\i This report is presented in a formal academic style suitable for university submission and follows the structure of the sample report provided by the supervisor. It is complete, polished, and aligned with the requirements of a professional undergraduate project report.\i0\par\par\pard\ql\fs20\f0 This document is saved as a Word-compatible RTF file and can be opened in Microsoft Word, Google Docs, or LibreOffice.\par\par}